\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The centered Grassmann action of Osterwalder and Seiler}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Osterwalder and Seiler use a centered nearest-neighbor
Euclidean spinor action, a supplied unitary internal gauge
representation and a real mass, including zero. Their
Grassmann integral is specified by its paired top
coefficient. Terms whose neighboring site lies outside
the finite lattice are omitted, giving free boundaries
\citet{Section 2.2, (2.13)-(2.14), pp. 446-447}{osterwalder1978gauge}.}

\p{Their positive half contains field generators at
strictly positive time and bond observables with both
endpoints there. The reflection is antilinear, exchanges
the two field types, inserts the time Dirac element and
reverses Grassmann product order. Theorem 2.1 proves
reflection positivity for gauge-invariant observables in
that half under the full integrated gauge-plus-fermion
expectation. Its proof uses temporal radiation gauge and
a positive cone
\citet{(2.15) and Theorem 2.1, pp. 447-448}{osterwalder1978gauge}.}

\p{The shared order-reversing convention is a useful
comparison with the [cellular reflection](fdip-00XQ).
The centered spinor operator, time Dirac element, free
boundaries and gauge treatment still require a separate
correspondence. This source is neither a Wilson-fermion
action nor a periodic cochain-circle theorem.}
